        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(ios): add private HealthKit summaries",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nCloses #105590.\n\nModels can invoke paired-iPhone capabilities, but OpenClaw has no privacy-bounded way to provide basic HealthKit context. Users currently need to export broad Health data or involve another service to ask for a recent steps, sleep, resting-heart-rate, or workout summary.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- Adds one read-only `health.summary` node command with a closed period enum: `today`, `7d`, or `30d`.\n- Aggregates steps, asleep duration, average resting heart rate, and workout count/duration on the iPhone. The payload contains no raw samples, sources, devices, metadata, clinical records, or write surface.\n- Adds an off-by-default iOS control whose disclosure states that requested aggregates pass through the Gateway to the configured AI provider and may remain in chat history. HealthKit authorization is requested only from this user action, never during `node.invoke`.\n- Advertises the capability only while the OpenClaw sharing switch is enabled. HealthKit read denial remains indistinguishable from no readable data; the UI and payload do not claim otherwise.\n- Classifies `health.summary` as a dangerous node command, so operators must also add it to `gateway.nodes.allowCommands` and approve the changed node surface.\n- Adds HealthKit purpose strings, entitlements, App Store signing/profile/IPA validation, review notes, product metadata, and operator documentation.\n\nThe current [Apple Developer Program License Agreement](https://developer.apple.com/support/terms/apple-developer-program-license-agreement/) permits HealthKit use for health/motion/fitness purposes when clear in marketing and UI, with prior express consent for third-party disclosure to provide that service. This implementation is constrained to that contract. It excludes advertising, data mining, diagnosis, medical advice, background ingestion, arbitrary queries, and writes.\n\nConfig/default surface metric: one dangerous built-in node command added (`health.summary`). It is excluded from runtime defaults and requires an explicit allowlist entry; existing users remain unaffected.\n\n## User Impact\n\nAfter enabling Health Summaries in **Settings -> Permissions -> Privacy & Access**, configuring the Gateway allowlist, and re-approving the iPhone node, a model can invoke:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"invoke\",\n  \"node\": \"My iPhone\",\n  \"invokeCommand\": \"health.summary\",\n  \"invokeParamsJson\": \"{\\\"period\\\":\\\"7d\\\"}\"\n}\n```\n\nBefore an App Store release, the release owner must update the public privacy policy and App Store Connect privacy details for Health & Fitness aggregates shared with the user's configured AI provider.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs run --id tbx_01kxbss7w55nv65w1gdb0093fv -- env OPENCLAW_CHECK_CHANGED_REMOTE_CHILD=1 OPENCLAW_CHANGED_LANES_RAW_SYNC=1 corepack pnpm check:changed`\n  - passed in 10m24s on Blacksmith Testbox\n  - formatting and Plugin SDK API baseline\n  - core, core-test, and root-test TypeScript typechecks\n  - exhaustive core/apps/scripts lint available on Linux: zero warnings/errors\n  - 188 supporting tests plus database/import-cycle/runtime/pairing guards\n  - SwiftLint correctly deferred to macOS CI\n- Focused Testbox proof: 114 assertions passed across node-command policy, security audit, App Store signing plan, and IPA entitlement validation.\n- `scripts/format-swift.sh ios`: passed, 0/289 files need formatting.\n- `xcodegen generate --spec apps/ios/project.yml --project apps/ios`: passed.\n- Fresh autoreview: no findings after checking the current Apple agreement.\n- `git diff --check`: passed.\n\nProof gap before merge: Xcode 26 iOS build/tests and SwiftLint require the macOS `ios-build` PR lane. Live source-blind HealthKit behavior remains blocked without a signed iPhone containing synthetic tester-owned Health data; personal Health data was not used.\n",
